# CUDA template suite, Windows 11 + RTX 3060, 2026-11-27 21:09
#
# What this is: templates/minimal-cuda-ofx/test/run-tests.bat Release, end to end
# and from a clean configure - the artifact (7 cubins), the four-step runner, the
# six GPU harness stages on the real device, three contract passes and the
# execution-mode truth table.
#
# Machine: NVIDIA GeForce RTX 3060, compute capability 8.6, 28 SMs, 12287 MiB.
# CUDA toolkit 13.0 (C:\Program Files\NVIDIA GPU Computing Toolkit\CUDA\v13.0),
# Ninja generator because the installed Visual Studio has no NVIDIA build
# customisations, MSVC 19.44.
#
# Result: all Windows checks passed, exit 0. This run's throughput block at
# 1080p / 4K / 8K: vec4 0.218 / 0.885 / 3.504 ms (303.9 / 299.9 / 303.0 GB/s),
# scalar within 2% of vec4, pageable copy 7.633 / 29.288 / 113.598 ms.
#
# Why it is a second transcript for the same day: the 18:24 run in
# cuda-suite.log measured 0.314 / 1.037 / 3.416 ms (211 / 256 / 311 GB/s) with the
# card busy with something else. Both conclude the same thing - the kernel is
# bandwidth-bound, vec4 buys nothing over scalar, the copy is the expensive part -
# and this one, at ~300 GB/s, is the one that agrees with the field test's
# 0.209 ms / 317 GB/s. Two runs, one conclusion; the difference between them is
# the machine's load, not the code.
#
# What changed in the source since the 18:24 run: Plugin.cpp's recordPath() now
# writes the CUDA stream per render into $MCOFX_PATH_LOG ("stream=<pointer|none|
# n/a>"). The suite does not set that variable, so this transcript is unchanged by
# it - but it is the build that produced the host-stream evidence of
# resolve-cuda-stream.log.
#
# The body below is the log verbatim, apart from two bytes: the console wrote its
# umlauts as cp850 and this file is UTF-8, so a reader and every grep in the tree
# agree.
#

